# Trivy suppressions.
#
# Rules for this file:
#   - Every entry states WHY it cannot simply be upgraded, and carries an
#     expiry (`exp:`) so it fails the pipeline again instead of silently
#     becoming permanent. An entry with no expiry is not acceptable here.
#   - Suppress only when the fix is genuinely blocked upstream. Anything
#     fixable by bumping a version belongs in the relevant pyproject.toml,
#     not here - see the "transitive-only dependencies" blocks in
#     api/pyproject.toml and core_ml/pyproject.toml for that pattern.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-69247 - cryptography 49.0.0 (HIGH)
#
# Blocked upstream, not by a choice made here: every published mlflow release
# (3.15.2 is the latest as of this writing) declares `cryptography<50,>=43.0.0`,
# so no resolvable dependency set exists that pairs mlflow with the fixed
# cryptography 50.0.0. Pinning cryptography 50 makes `poetry lock` fail
# outright.
#
# Exposure here is limited: cryptography is a transitive dependency reached
# only through mlflow's own client code paths (artifact-store TLS and token
# handling). This project never calls the affected primitives directly, and the
# tracking server it talks to is inside the VPC, not internet-facing.
#
# Remove this entry as soon as mlflow widens the constraint - re-check with:
#   curl -s https://pypi.org/pypi/mlflow/json | grep -o 'cryptography[^"]*'
CVE-2026-69247 exp:2026-11-30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0132 - "S3 encryption should use Customer Managed Keys"
#
# The artifacts bucket is encrypted at rest with SSE-S3 / AES-256
# (terraform/s3.tf). A customer-managed KMS key would add per-request KMS
# charges and a key policy to maintain, and buys nothing here: there is no
# regulatory requirement to hold the key material, no cross-account access, and
# no key-rotation-on-demand story this project needs. Revisit if this ever
# stores customer PII rather than model artifacts.
AWS-0132 exp:2027-06-30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0031 - "ECR image tags shouldn't be mutable"
#
# What actually pins a deployment here is the immutable commit SHA that
# `kustomize edit set image` writes into
# kubernetes/overlays/production/kustomization.yaml - never a floating tag. CI
# additionally pushes `:latest` as a human convenience, and IMMUTABLE tags make
# re-pushing that fail the build. The GitOps flow already gives the property
# this rule is protecting.
AWS-0031 exp:2027-06-30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0104 - "Security group rule allows unrestricted egress"
#
# terraform/vpc.tf's EKS node security group. Worker nodes must reach the EKS
# control plane, ECR, S3, Secrets Manager and the upstream registries images
# are pulled from. Constraining that to explicit prefixes would mean VPC
# endpoints for every one of those services - real cost and operational
# surface for a portfolio-scale deployment. *Ingress* is already restricted:
# the RDS security group only accepts 5432 from this exact group.
AWS-0104 exp:2027-06-30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0040 / AWS-0041 - EKS public endpoint, open to 0.0.0.0/0
#
# Deliberate for this deployment, and narrower than it looks: CI never touches
# the cluster (delivery is GitOps - ArgoCD pulls from inside the VPC), so the
# only consumer of the public endpoint is the operator running kubectl from a
# dynamic residential IP. Authentication is IAM (EKS access entries), not a
# network boundary, and the worker nodes and RDS instance both sit in private
# subnets.
#
# terraform/variables.tf exposes `cluster_endpoint_public_access_cidrs` so this
# can be narrowed to a fixed office/VPN egress IP without editing eks.tf; a
# production deployment should either do that or set
# cluster_endpoint_public_access = false and reach the API through a bastion.
AWS-0040 exp:2027-06-30
AWS-0041 exp:2027-06-30
